so welcome back everyone uh now that we  understand what blockchains are used for  let's talk a little bit about how do we  actually  protect data on the blockchain so it's  not publicly available to the whole  world  so again just to review if we look at a  bitcoin transaction  you realize that the source addresses  are written in the clear on the  blockchain and all the amounts are  written in the clear on the blockchain  as well so everyone can see who is  paying who  one thing that i need to point out is  this may look like it's hiding the  identity of the pair and it's hiding the  identity of who's being paid but in  reality there's actually a lot of work  to show that in fact it's very difficult  to figure out the physical identity  associated with this address there are  many papers to do it and in fact there  are companies that analyze the  blockchain and map these addresses to  physical entities so really it's uh  there's no privacy here right in a sense  you know exactly who paid who and how  much at any given time  so this is a problem for businesses and  that you can see who the pair is who the  payees and what the amounts were in  particular if uh  stanford university wanted to pay my  salary in bitcoin everybody in the world  will be able to see exactly what my  salary is so that's kind of a problem um  similarly in a supply chain case you  know if ford wanted to buy tires from  its supplier and pay in bitcoin  everybody would see exactly how much  ford is paying for its for its tires so  that is kind of unusable by businesses  it's kind of a mismatch with business  needs and so the question is what can we  do  and it turns out there's a beautiful  beautiful idea  that allows you to actually retain the  property of public verifiability  but do so in a way that does not violate  privacy yeah so we preserve privacy  so this is an idea that's called  confidential transactions and the idea  is rather than writing the amounts on  the blockchain in the clear  what we're going to do is we're going to  replace all these amounts by what's  called the commitment to the amounts  okay so what is a commitment a  commitment is basically  a way to represent data in a way that no  one can know what the data is  but the person who wrote the data can no  longer change it yeah so  they're committed to a particular value  even though no one knows what the value  is so you know it's one of these things  where i can't actually give a talk  without doing a little bit of math so  you can see that the amount that was  written was five three three and so on  and so forth instead of writing five  three three in the clear what we're  gonna do is we're gonna write the number  five three three instead of sort of an  exponent and then we're going to hide  this value by blinding it with another  value so all that is sort of what's  called a cryptographic commitment i'm  not going to get into the details of  that i'll just tell you there's a  standard cryptographic commitment called  the pedersen commitment  again you can look this up if you want  to read more about how pedestrian  commitments work but effectively the way  we commit to a value is uh we put the  value in the exponent and then we  blinded by a random uh by a random value  so that the commitment itself the  commitment is this uh number that we get  over here the commitment itself reveals  nothing about the value v  but at the same time there is no way to  commit to a different value so the  committer is bound to the number v  great so now basically the the amounts  are no longer visible on the blockchain  um and yet all the uh amounts are  committed to so we can all the the  people who wrote these transactions  cannot change the amounts but there's no  way for them for anyone to know what the  amounts are  so this is great  unfortunately it looks like now we lose  the ability to verify to validate  transactions right so normally when we  validate a transaction we have to check  that the sum of the inputs is equal to  the sum of the outputs right that's what  transaction validity means yeah the  amount of money that came in is equal to  the amount of money came out plus  potentially a fee  and that basically guarantees that no  money gets minted and no money is lost  but now that all the values are hidden  how do we know that this equation  actually holds  everything is hidden so how do we verify  it well this is one of these beautiful  applications of cryptography  where we're going to use a mechanism  called a zero knowledge proof  uh which we discussed at quite a bit of  length in our in our crypto courses but  here i'm just going to assert there is  such a thing called the zero knowledge  proof that allows us to prove that this  equation star actually holds without  actually revealing anything else about  the equation so anyone looking at the  transaction can verify that the equality  holds but they learn nothing about the  actual values that's what a zero  knowledge proof allows you to do i know  it sounds kind of magical but there is a  way to actually do this and do it quite  efficiently yeah so what we do we prove  in zero knowledge is first of all the  equality holds and second of all that in  fact all the numbers are positive yeah  and positive i mean in this range it's  kind of interesting if you were able to  kind of convince someone that the  equality holds but one of the output  values was negative  that turns out to basically you can use  that to mint money out of thin air and  nobody no one would know that that's  actually happening so proving that all  the values are positive is kind of  fundamental to the security of the  scheme  oddly i can tell you that proving the  the equality is quite easy proving that  the values are positive is much harder  and there's a lot of work on making that  more efficient including some of our own  work that basically shows how to  implement confidential transactions in a  very efficient way  okay so that's one way so now we've  hidden the amounts so when stanford pays  my salary basically on in bitcoin on the  blockchain everybody will know that  stanford is paying my salary but they  will not know what my salary is even  though they can confirm that all these  transactions are perfectly valid okay so  even though it's kind of fascinating  that even though everything is written  in the clear and we have public  verifiability we can still hide data  from the public eye  well it turns out we can hide even more  from the public eye in fact there are  blockchains like for example zcash where  not only are the amounts hidden but in  fact everything is hidden so even the  identity of the payer the identity of  the payee all of that is hidden so when  stanford pays my salary if they choose  to use zcash people will not even know  that i am that stanford is paying my  salary um similarly well when ford buys  his tires from its supplier if they use  cash nobody will know even though who  the supplier is because everything is  hidden on the blockchain yeah so again  it's fascinating this can all be done  yeah so we have public verifiability  everyone can verify that the blockchain  um  only contains valid transactions money  is not created out of thin air money is  not lost nobody's double spending their  their their funds  so we have public verifiability and yet  all this information is completely  hidden and that's and by the way i  should mention there's another  private blockchain called monero that  has similar goals  so the question you want you might ask  is how is all this possible and the  answer is again using cryptography magic  i wish i could explain it to you it's  really quite beautiful  but it uses sort of more advanced zero  knowledge techniques in particular some  something called a zero knowledge snark  snark stands for succinct  non-interactive argument of knowledge  it's a good name zero knowledge snark  you can look it up  you can read a little bit more about how  how zk snarks work um  but i should say that we also covered in  our courses so if you're interested in  seeing that then please take some of our  future courses  it's a fascinating area and  it's quite a strong capability as you  can imagine in the context of  blockchains because again it allows us  to preserve public verifiability without  revealing information on the public  blockchain so a very useful tool  all right so that's all i wanted to say  about privacy on the blockchain i just  wanted to make it clear that just before  because the blockchain is public doesn't  mean that all the data is public  and the next thing i want to talk about  is essentially the mechanics of how  mining actually works yeah so we haven't  really talked about what the miners do  so now we're going to kind of move  around in our you know a little survey  and talk a little bit about how mining  works in practice
 

